# Golden test fixtures are byte-compared against harness output, and the harness always
# writes LF (tools/banktest/run-banktest.sh and realrom-test.sh both normalize CR away
# before writing). Without this, core.autocrlf=true rewrites a golden to CRLF on checkout
# and every subsequent `check` reports a WHOLE-FILE diff for a file whose content is
# byte-identical -- the diagnostic trap bead grm-g73 records, where a handful of changed
# lines present as "78 deleted, 101 added" and badly overstate an apparent regression.
*.dump text eol=lf

# The real-ROM manifest is tab-separated with an optional trailing column, so *which*
# field is last varies row to row. Under core.autocrlf=true that field carries a trailing
# CR into a path or a headless argument, and the failure presents as "golden not found"
# for a file that plainly exists -- a trap worth spending one line to close rather than
# re-deriving from the symptom. tools/banktest/realrom-test.sh keeps a cheap `tr -d '\r'`
# on its input as a second layer, for a working-tree copy an editor rewrote after checkout.
*.tsv text eol=lf

# Committed Gradle wrapper (bead grm-ycv). gradlew is invoked directly (its own shebang),
# so it must always check out with LF -- core.autocrlf=true would otherwise rewrite it to
# CRLF and break the "#!/bin/sh" interpreter line ("bad interpreter"). gradlew.bat is a
# native Windows batch file and wants CRLF regardless of platform.
/gradlew text eol=lf
/gradlew.bat text eol=crlf
